# 19. Optimize for Information Gain

When choosing what to implement next, prioritize experiments that answer high-impact unknowns.

For example:

If we don't know whether UFS expert streaming is viable, testing a 120B MoE streaming prototype may provide more useful information than spending two days optimizing a 9B dense model.

If we don't know whether the GPU backend can outperform CPU, build a tiny GEMM benchmark first.

If we don't know whether the NPU can execute useful transformer operators, build a capability probe.

Always ask:

> "What experiment will reduce the most uncertainty for the least effort?"

---

# 20. Do Not Get Trapped by Conventional Architecture

If the conventional path is:

```text
RAM → all weights → CPU → inference
```

but hardware constraints make it impractical, investigate:

```text
UFS → streamed weights → cache → compute
```

If CPU-only is too slow:

```text
CPU + GPU
```

If GPU support is incomplete:

```text
CPU + selective GPU
```

If NPU cannot run the full graph:

```text
NPU for supported operators
+
CPU fallback
```

If one model cannot fit:

```text
Multiple quantization levels
+
streaming
+
dynamic model loading
```

If a standard API is unavailable:

```text
AOSP
+
NDK
+
vendor libraries
+
system interfaces
+
experimental probing
```

Explore the engineering space before concluding that something is impossible.

